# findings.baseline — every row the design space currently reports, named once.
#
# THE GATE FIRES ON THE DIFFERENCE, not on the rows. A new row is red because
# nobody has looked at it; a vanished row is red because the baseline has
# stopped being true and a fix deserves a fixture. Same shape as
# tests/logos/bc_admits.ledger and logos_00_census_pin.
#
# Format:  <disposition> <question> <the question's own columns>
#
#   FALSE      triaged and refuted, with a one-variable control. Not a defect.
#   OPEN       confirmed live, repro proven, not yet fixed. See docs/baghunt/.
#   NARROW     a deliberately narrow subset, with the reason stated at the site.
#   UNTRIAGED  nobody has looked. NOT a verdict — a debt, and the count is the
#              measure of it. This gate is a RATCHET until that count is small:
#              it stops the pile growing silently, and becomes a ctest gate when
#              the pile is gone.
#
# ⚠ DO NOT trim rows to make the gate quiet. A row removed without a fixture is
# a finding deleted, which is the one move this whole apparatus exists to make
# impossible.
# WHAT THE DISPOSITIONS RECORD TODAY. Only rows actually established are marked;
# everything else stays UNTRIAGED, which is honest rather than tidy.
#   FALSE   4  — each refuted with a one-variable control, one (gen_recv_struct_inner)
#                by a gdb breakpoint proving the kinds arrive and are answered elsewhere
#   NARROW  4  — deliberate subsets, each with the reason stated at its own site
#   OPEN    2  — mark_moved_in_expr_recursive (walker incomplete, live hole closed
#                elsewhere) and visit_stmt/DerefWrite/check_live, whose repro and
#                three failed repair attempts are in
#                docs/baghunt/open_derefwrite_shared_borrow.md
